.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_base_id
returns the type of
.Fa pkey .
For example an RSA key will return
.Sy EVP_PKEY_RSA .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_id
returns the actual NID associated with
.Fa pkey
only if the
.Fa pkey
type isn't implemented just in a
.Xr provider 7 .
Historically keys using the same algorithm could use different NIDs.
For example an RSA key could use the NIDs corresponding to the NIDs
.Sy NID_rsaEncryption
(equivalent to
.Sy EVP_PKEY_RSA )
or
.Sy NID_rsa
(equivalent to
.Sy EVP_PKEY_RSA2 Ns ).
The use of alternative non-standard NIDs is now rare so
.Sy EVP_PKEY_RSA2
et al are not often seen in practice.
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_id
returns -1
.Pf ( Sy EVP_PKEY_KEYMGMT )
if the
.Fa pkey
is only implemented in a
.Xr provider 7 .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_type
returns the underlying type of the NID
.Fa type .
For example EVP_PKEY_type(EVP_PKEY_RSA2) will return
.Sy EVP_PKEY_RSA .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_DH
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_EC_KEY
set the key referenced by
.Fa pkey
to
.Fa key .
These functions are deprecated.
Applications should instead use
.Xr EVP_PKEY_fromdata 3 .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_DH ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_EC_KEY ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_POLY1305
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_SIPHASH
set the referenced key to
.Fa key
however these use the supplied
.Fa key
internally and so
.Fa key
will be freed when the parent
.Fa pkey
is freed.
These macros are deprecated.
Applications should instead read an EVP_PKEY directly using the
OSSL_DECODER APIs (see
.Xr OSSL_DECODER_CTX_new_for_pkey 3 Ns ),
or construct an EVP_PKEY from data using
.Xr EVP_PKEY_fromdata 3 .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DH
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_EC_KEY
return the referenced key in
.Fa pkey
or NULL if the key is not of the correct type.
The returned key must be freed after use.
These functions are deprecated.
Applications should instead use the EVP_PKEY directly where possible.
If access to the low level key parameters is required then applications
should use
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get_params 3
and other similar functions.
To write an EVP_PKEY out use the OSSL_ENCODER APIs (see
.Xr OSSL_ENCODER_CTX_new_for_pkey 3 Ns ).
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_hmac ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_poly1305 ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_siphash ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_DH
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_EC_KEY
return the referenced key in
.Fa pkey
or NULL if the key is not of the correct type.
The reference count of the returned key is
.Sy not
incremented and so the key must not be freed after use.
These functions are deprecated.
Applications should instead use the EVP_PKEY directly where possible.
If access to the low level key parameters is required then applications
should use
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get_params 3
and other similar functions.
To write an EVP_PKEY out use the OSSL_ENCODER APIs (see
.Xr OSSL_ENCODER_CTX_new_for_pkey 3 Ns ).
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0
returns a pointer to the legacy key or NULL if the key is not legacy.
.Pp
Note that if an EVP_PKEY was not constructed using one of the deprecated
functions such as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_DH
or
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_EC_KEY ,
or via the similarly named
.Sy EVP_PKEY_assign
macros described above then the internal key will be managed by a
provider (see
.Xr provider 7 Ns ).
In that case the key returned by
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DH ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_EC_KEY ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_hmac ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_poly1305 ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_siphash ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_DH
or
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_EC_KEY
will be a cached copy of the provider's key.
Subsequent updates to the provider's key will not be reflected back in
the cached copy, and updates made by an application to the returned key
will not be reflected back in the provider's key.
Subsequent calls to
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DH
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_EC_KEY
will always return the cached copy returned by the first call.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_engine
returns a reference to the ENGINE handling
.Fa pkey .
This function is deprecated.
Applications should use providers instead of engines (see
.Xr provider 7
for details).
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_engine
sets the ENGINE handling
.Fa pkey
to
.Fa engine .
It must be called after the key algorithm and components are set up.
If
.Fa engine
does not include an
.Sy EVP_PKEY_METHOD
for
.Fa pkey
an error occurs.
This function is deprecated.
Applications should use providers instead of engines (see
.Xr provider 7
for details).
.Sh WARNINGS
The following functions are only reliable with
.Vt EVP_PKEY Ns s
that have been assigned an internal key with
.Xr EVP_PKEY_assign_* 3 :
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_id ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_base_id ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_type
.Pp
For EVP_PKEY key type checking purposes,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_is_a 3
is more generic.
.Pp
For purposes of retrieving the name of the
.Vt EVP_PKEY
the function
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get0_type_name 3
is more generally useful.
.Pp
The keys returned from the functions
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_DH
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_EC_KEY
were changed to have a "const" return type in OpenSSL 3.0. As described
above the keys returned may be cached copies of the key held in a
provider.
Due to this, and unlike in earlier versions of OpenSSL, they should be
considered read-only copies of the key.
Updates to these keys will not be reflected back in the provider side
key.
The
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DH
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_EC_KEY
functions were not changed to have a "const" return type in order that
applications can "free" the return value.
However applications should still consider them as read-only copies.
.Sh NOTES
In accordance with the OpenSSL naming convention the key obtained from
or assigned to the
.Fa pkey
using the
.Sy 1
functions must be freed as well as
.Fa pkey .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_DH ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_EC_KEY ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_POLY1305
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_SIPHASH
are implemented as macros.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_EC_KEY
looks at the curve name id to determine if the passed
.Vt EC_KEY
is an
.Xr SM2 7
key, and will set the
.Vt EVP_PKEY
type to
.Sy EVP_PKEY_SM2
in that case, instead of
.Sy EVP_PKEY_EC .
.Pp
Most applications wishing to know a key type will simply call
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_base_id
and will not care about the actual type: which will be identical in
almost all cases.
.Pp
Previous versions of this document suggested using
EVP_PKEY_type(pkey->type) to determine the type of a key.
Since
.Vt EVP_PKEY
is now opaque this is no longer possible: the equivalent is
EVP_PKEY_get_base_id(pkey).
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_engine
is typically used by an ENGINE returning an HSM key as part of its
routine to load a private key.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_DH
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_EC_KEY
return 1 for success or 0 for failure.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_DH
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get1_EC_KEY
return the referenced key or NULL if an error occurred.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_RSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_DSA ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_DH ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_EC_KEY ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_POLY1305
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_assign_SIPHASH
return 1 for success and 0 for failure.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_base_id ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_id
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_type
return a key type or
.Sy NID_undef
(equivalently
.Sy EVP_PKEY_NONE )
on error.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_set1_engine
returns 1 for success and 0 for failure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr EVP_PKEY_new 3 ,
.Xr SM2 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn EVP_PKEY_id
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_base_id
functions were renamed to include
.Qo Li get Qc
in their names in OpenSSL 3.0, respectively.
The old names are kept as non-deprecated alias macros.
.Pp
